CORE
main.c
--unwind 2 --rounds 4 --por --allow-pointer-unsoundness
^EXIT=0$
^SIGNAL=0$
^VERIFICATION SUCCESSFUL$
--
^VERIFICATION FAILED$
--
A shared object is carried through the lazy chain once per round at its full
width. A pthread_mutex_t is the real 24-byte glibc union, 192 bits, but CBMC's
model reads and writes exactly one byte of it (__CPROVER_mutex_t is a signed
char) through a type-punned pointer, so field_sensitivityt -- which splits
ID_member and ID_index only -- never separates that byte out. Narrowing the
chain to the bytes some read observes is on by default; --no-narrow-shared
restores the full-width chain.

Runs with no flag on purpose: this is the default path.

Discriminating in the direction that matters. Mutual exclusion is carried
entirely by that one byte, so a slice taken at the wrong offset, or a chain
that lost the byte, would let both threads hold the lock, in_cs would reach 2,
and this safe program would report a violation it does not have. Verified by
mutation: with the two pthread_mutex_lock/unlock calls removed the same program
reports VERIFICATION FAILED.

Narrowing takes this formula from 63131 clauses to 26427, same verdict.
